<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>DeltaZulu.Kql.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Azure.Kusto.Language" VersionOverride="[12.4.1]" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="MSTest.TestAdapter" />
    <PackageReference Include="MSTest.TestFramework" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The CEILING of the version span (CON-0006). The sibling project tests the
      shipped assembly against the 9.2.0 floor; this one recompiles the library
      SOURCES against 12.4.1 and runs the same conformance suite over them.

      Source inclusion rather than ProjectReference is deliberate and necessary.
      The library compiles against 9.2.0, so its public signatures carry the
      assembly identity 'Kusto.Language, Version=9.2.0.0'; a project referencing
      it while overriding the package to 12.4.1 cannot resolve those signatures at
      compile time. (At runtime the mismatch is fine — .NET resolves by simple name
      and a higher version satisfies a lower reference — which is exactly why the
      minimum-not-exact pin works in Platform's process.)

      Compiling the sources here is the stronger test anyway: it proves the library
      only uses API that EXISTS in 12.4.1, which a binary reference cannot tell you.

      Both source sets are linked, never copied. Two copies would drift, and a
      conformance suite that differs between the versions it compares proves nothing.
    -->
    <Compile Include="../../src/DeltaZulu.Kql/*.cs" LinkBase="Library" />
    <Compile Include="../DeltaZulu.Kql.Tests/*.cs" LinkBase="Conformance" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
